{-
   Mutual exclusion, as one interface - Analysis-Atomics.md §7.

   Two types in this library exclude, and they are the same operation with different answers to one
   question: what to do when the lock is already held. A `SpinLock` tries again; a `Mutex` stops.
   Everything else about them - that `lock` waits until it holds it, that `unlock` publishes what was
   written under it, that `tryLock` decides and acts in one step, that `isHeld` is advisory - is the
   same statement about both, and a caller that only needs exclusion should not have to care which it
   was handed.

   So it is a class rather than two families of identically named functions. That is not only tidier:
   two `lock`s declared as plain functions are two plain functions of one name, and a file importing
   both modules could not call either - which is exactly the state this replaces, and it is the state
   any *third* lock would have put us back into.

   § What is deliberately not here

   **Nothing about waiting.** A `Condition` takes a `Mutex` and not a `Lock(a)`, because releasing
   the lock and blocking has to be one step the waiter's kernel primitive knows about - a spin lock
   has nothing to release *to*, and a condition over one would spin holding nothing.

   **No reader/writer form.** `RwMutex` has two of everything and is not this interface with more
   methods on it; a reader lock and a writer lock are two locks that share a word.

   **No fairness or reentrancy promise**, because neither implementation makes one. A class is a
   statement about what its methods mean, and "some instance might be fair" is not a meaning a
   caller can use.

   § Choosing between them

   The class makes a data structure able to be *parameterised* by its lock, which is the reason to
   have it - a bucketed map wants one lock per bucket and its author, not this library, knows which.
   It also makes it easy to write the wrong one, so: a `SpinLock` is right only when the critical
   section is shorter than a context switch and the holder cannot be descheduled inside it. Anything
   that allocates, takes another lock, or calls into the operating system wants the `Mutex`.
-}
import Native

pub class Lock(a):
  -- One attempt, which never waits. Answers whether this thread now holds it, and is the only one
  -- of these that can be asked without committing to anything.
  fn tryLock(self: a) -> Bool

  -- Waits until it holds it. How it waits is the whole difference between the instances.
  fn lock(self: a) -> {}

  -- Releases it. Unlocking a lock this thread does not hold is a program error and is not checked
  -- by either instance.
  fn unlock(self: a) -> {}

  -- Whether anybody holds it, right now. **Advisory**: the answer is stale by the time it is read,
  -- and deciding to lock from it is the check-then-act race `tryLock` exists to prevent.
  fn isHeld(self: a) -> Bool

{-
   The scoped form, written once over the class rather than once per instance.

   A `lens` is what makes the release automatic: the block below the call is the critical section and
   the unlock runs on the way out of it, including a `return` that leaves the enclosing function from
   inside. That needs no destructor and nothing written at either site.

   A generic function beside the class rather than a member with a default, and the reason is not a
   restriction: a class `lens fn` may carry one, and calling it works - see LensClass.yana. It is
   what a member would be *for*. A member is a position an instance may answer differently, and
   these three lines are the same three lines for every lock that can be written against this
   interface: waiting is `lock`'s business, and a lock with a scoped primitive of its own would
   still release by unlocking. Nothing here would ever be overridden.

   And a position costs. A member is a slot in every witness, which an erased call loads through -
   so a default nobody overrides is one more indirection per instance for a body that was going to
   be the same body. Outside the class it is one generic function, specialized per lock exactly as
   it would have been, and the class stays the four operations that actually differ.

   The explicit pair stays public because a lock taken in one function and released in another is a
   real algorithm rather than a mistake.
-}
pub lens fn (Lock(a)) withLock(self: a) -> {}:
    lock(self)
    yield {}
    unlock(self)
